export const debate = {"id":"craig-atkins-does-god-exist-2011","number":"166","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","assessmentRubric":"Slugfester Reassessment Rubric v2","title":"William Lane Craig vs Peter Atkins: Does God Exist?","label":"Theistic arguments and scientific naturalism","date":"2026-08-25","duration":"1 hr 53 min","youtubeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ssq-S5M8wsY","motion":"Does God exist as the transcendent personal cause and moral foundation of reality?","sourceNote":"Based on the complete debate transcript. Summaries are condensed for clarity, and quotations are kept brief and faithful to the source.","scoringNote":"Scores are AI-generated estimates of argumentative performance and are intended as transparent, revisable judgments rather than final verdicts.","summary":"Craig advances cumulative cosmological, moral, and resurrection arguments, then answers naturalistic objections concerning causality, evolution, suffering, miracles, and scientific method.","quotes":{"pro":{"text":"the universe began to exist is a religiously neutral statement","context":"Craig distinguishes a scientifically informed premise about cosmic beginning from the subsequent philosophical inference carrying theological significance."},"con":{"text":"physical laws which are summaries of observed Behavior come into existence","context":"Atkins uses this characterization of physical law to challenge extending familiar causal relations beyond the universe whose behavior they summarize."}},"sides":{"pro":{"name":"Classical theism","speaker":"William Lane Craig","color":"teal"},"con":{"name":"Scientific naturalism","speaker":"Peter Atkins","color":"coral"}},"score":{"pro":83,"con":73},"sections":[{"sectionId":"cosmic-origin-causality","title":"Cosmic Origin, Causality, and a Personal First Cause","timebox":"6:02–59:28","score":{"pro":82,"con":78},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-cosmic-finite-past-personal-cause","time":"6:02","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Because the universe began to exist, it requires an uncaused, timeless, immaterial, transcendent cause, which Craig further characterizes as personal.","score":81,"critique":"Strongest feature: The argument presents a recoverable conditional syllogism, identifies a transcendent cause as its conclusion, and connects that conclusion to the cosmic-origin burden with clear metaphysical attributes. Principal limitation: The transition from an uncaused, timeless, immaterial cause to a personal agent remains compressed, while the excerpt supplies neither empirical defense of a finite beginning nor philosophical defense of universal causal application. Live burden: The case must establish both premises against an infinite sequence and universe-bound causality, then provide an independent reason why agency better explains timeless origination than an impersonal condition. Locked score: The strong band reflects excellent relevance and formal clarity, moderated by incomplete displayed warrant, implicit qualification, and the materially important personal-cause bridge.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-cosmos-c005-causality-arises-with-universe","time":"31:29","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Because physical laws and causal relations summarize behavior within a universe and arise with it, an original universe need not possess a prior cause.","score":79,"critique":"Strongest feature: This reply confronts both decisive components of the cosmological inference, distinguishing an original universe from daughter universes and questioning whether causal language derived from observed physical behavior legitimately applies before physical reality. Principal limitation: Describing laws as summaries does not establish that every causal principle is merely physical, so the argument leaves open the affirmative's stronger metaphysical claim that contingent beginnings require explanation independently of temporal mechanisms. Live burden: Atkins must justify the transition from causality's intra-universe formulation to the exclusion of any transphysical explanatory principle, while Craig must independently defend applicability beyond the universe. Locked score: The strong band recognizes unusually direct diagnostic contact, high responsiveness, and central relevance, while retaining deductions for modal instability, incomplete engagement with metaphysical causation, and slightly excessive force.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-cosmic-nothing-zero-energy-cause","time":"48:55","role":"Major direct reply","words":"A zero balance of positive and negative energy remains an existing reality, not nothing, and therefore does not eliminate the origin question.","score":82,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply draws the sharp ontological distinction between zero net energy and nonbeing, observing that balancing positive and negative quantities describes an existing physical reality rather than literal nothing. Principal limitation: It concentrates on zero-energy formulation rather than Atkins's contention that causal laws arise with the universe, and does not establish that transcendent causation follows from exposing this conceptual mismatch. Live burden: The affirmative must show why causality applies to cosmic origination, why net-zero physical reality cannot be explanatorily basic, and why explanation is personal rather than merely structural. Locked score: The strong band rewards conceptual precision and responsiveness, while recognizing overstated necessity, incomplete causal warrant, and partial engagement with naturalistic diagnosis of universe-bound causality.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-cosmos-c03-infinite-universe-sequence","time":"57:06","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Finite ages assigned to individual universes do not exclude an infinite sequence with no beginning, and evidence must determine whether that sequence is finite.","score":76,"critique":"Strongest feature: This reply sharply distinguishes the measured age of an individual universe from the possible duration of a universe-generating sequence, thereby contacting the finite-past premise without pretending that present evidence conclusively establishes infinitude. Principal limitation: Its categorical dismissal of philosophical argument is unsupported, because conceptual objections to an actually infinite temporal sequence could remain relevant even when empirical evidence is also necessary, and it leaves the zero-energy issue untouched. Live burden: Atkins must provide evidence or a model making the infinite sequence more than abstractly available and answer philosophical objections on their merits rather than excluding them methodologically. Locked score: The strong band rewards disciplined possibility-level calibration, stable scope, clear qualification, and direct central contact, while recognizing incomplete responsiveness, modest contrary evidence, and insufficient justification for evidential exclusivity.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"objective-morality-grounding","title":"Objective Morality and Its Foundation","timebox":"15:42–74:17","score":{"pro":84,"con":72},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-morality-objective-values-divine-ground","time":"15:42","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Objective moral values and duties bind independently of human belief, and their existence requires God as a transcendent moral foundation.","score":81,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move clearly defines objective moral values as binding independently of human belief and connects their reality to the affirmative route's claim about God's moral character and transcendent foundation. Principal limitation: The categorical conditional excludes every nontheistic ground without displaying an argument against moral realism or another account capable of distinguishing objective validity from individual preference. Live burden: The affirmative must defend mind-independent duties and the stronger grounding thesis that such duties require a divine nature, rather than asserting that evolutionary genealogy cannot supply their authority. Locked score: The strong band recognizes a precise, highly relevant constructive framework, while withholding greater credit because its exclusive-foundation inference is implicit, uncertainty is unacknowledged, and supporting warrant is thin.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-morality-c010-evolutionary-social-origin","time":"41:03","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Moral distinctions emerged through evolutionary history and consequence-sensitive human cognition, providing a natural account of morality without requiring a divine source or judge.","score":72,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply presents a naturalistic genealogy of moral distinctions, adds consequence-sensitive cognition to evolutionary competition, and challenges the claim that moral capacities require divine authorship or supervision. Principal limitation: It moves between explaining behavioral dispositions and grounding objectively binding validity, leaving unresolved the distinction between the causes of moral belief and whether beliefs track mind-independent obligations. Live burden: Atkins must supplement evolutionary genealogy with an account of normative authority, or argue that objective bindingness is unnecessary, without assuming that origins determine truth or justification. Locked score: The mixed band recognizes relevance, nuance, and explanatory support for moral conduct, while reflecting incomplete responsiveness, partly unstable terminology, an underdeveloped anti-foundational inference, and limited representation of the opposing premise.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-morality-evolution-validity-distinction","time":"71:56","role":"Diagnostic challenge","words":"Evolution may explain contingent moral behavior and preferences, but it does not thereby ground objectively binding, mind-independent moral values or duties.","score":87,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply isolates the genealogy-validity distinction: evolution can explain contingent moral attitudes and behaviors without explaining why duty is objectively true or binding independently of those attitudes. Principal limitation: Defeating Atkins's genealogy does not establish that only a transcendent divine foundation can ground moral truth, because naturalistic realism could add an independent ontology of reasons or values. Live burden: The affirmative must defend objective normativity, identify how divine nature grounds rather than merely commands it, and compare that account with the strongest nontheistic realist alternative instead of stopping at evolutionary contingency. Locked score: The very-strong band reflects precise diagnosis, complete contact, stable terminology, and charitable representation, moderated by the remaining exclusive-grounding bridge and limited displayed ontology.","tags":[]},"con":null}]},{"sectionId":"resurrection-miracle-inference","title":"Resurrection Evidence and Miracle Inference","timebox":"20:05–77:13","score":{"pro":81,"con":71},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-resurrection-historical-facts-inference","time":"20:05","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"The empty tomb, postmortem appearances, and origin of the disciples' belief are historical facts best explained by Jesus's divine resurrection.","score":81,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move offers a concrete abductive structure around the empty tomb, reported appearances, and the origin of resurrection belief, placing historical claims rather than bare faith at the center of the divine inference. Principal limitation: Majority recognition is invoked without displaying source independence, reliability, or disciplined comparison between resurrection and specific natural alternatives, so the claimed best explanation exceeds the presented documentation. Live burden: The case must establish each fact, assess partisan sources fairly, incorporate prior probability, and show that divine action predicts the combined evidence better than ordinary hypotheses. Locked score: The strong band rewards clarity, central relevance, and a coherent explanatory proposal, but remains limited by compressed historiography, implicit qualifications, and incomplete comparative testing.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-resurrection-c008-no-reliable-miracle","time":"38:34","role":"Major direct reply","words":"No reliable divine suspension of natural law has been established, while ordinary natural accounts remain more credible than resurrection as an explanation of reported events.","score":69,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply squarely challenges the resurrection route's indispensable evidential sufficiency, emphasizing that a singular divine intervention requires reliable support rather than acceptance merely because an event currently resists an ordinary explanation. Principal limitation: Its categorical assertion that such miracles have never been observed neither specifies the relevant historical domain nor examines the empty tomb, appearances, and belief-origin claims individually, thereby substituting a generalized denial for comparative source analysis. Live burden: Atkins must assess the particular testimony and proposed explanations under an articulated reliability standard, while allowing that historical evidence can sometimes support nonrepeatable events without direct observation. Locked score: The mixed band reflects central relevance and meaningful contact with miracle reliability, but also materially overstated calibration, incomplete fact-specific responsiveness, unstable evidential terminology, and limited charity.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-resurrection-probability-explanation","time":"74:14","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Considering background evidence and comparative explanatory virtues prevents prior improbability alone from excluding resurrection as the best explanation of the historical facts.","score":80,"critique":"Strongest feature: The response reframes miracle assessment as a comparative probability question involving background evidence and explanatory likelihoods, rather than allowing prior improbability to defeat historical evidence automatically. Principal limitation: The framework remains summarized, does not carefully order the relevant conditional comparisons, and leaves Atkins's attack on Gospel reliability and political interest substantially unanswered. Live burden: The affirmative must document the historical facts, test dependence and bias in the sources, specify natural alternatives, and show why resurrection has superior explanatory scope without assuming the theistic background it supports. Locked score: The strong band credits coherent probabilistic framing, central relevance, and charitable engagement with skepticism, while reflecting incomplete source analysis, partial responsiveness, and best-explanation force beyond the displayed comparison.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-resurrection-c06-extraordinary-evidence-absent","time":"65:23","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Resurrection requires extraordinary evidence, yet the Gospel reports are interested political constructions rather than sufficiently reliable records supporting such an exceptional conclusion.","score":73,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply contacts both the historical-warrant and miracle-inference components, insisting that an exceptionally improbable conclusion needs commensurately discriminating evidence and directly questioning whether interested Gospel narratives satisfy that requirement. Principal limitation: Neither extraordinary evidence nor political manifesto receives a defensible criterion, and partisanship alone cannot establish invention, dependence, or factual unreliability without detailed source comparison and engagement with the specific reported facts. Live burden: Atkins must articulate a consistent evidential threshold, demonstrate relevant deficiencies in testimony and transmission, and compare ordinary alternatives instead of treating theological interest as dispositive fabrication. Locked score: The mixed band acknowledges full structural responsiveness, central relevance, and a recoverable skeptical inference, but reflects thin documentary support, materially overstated calibration, partly unstable terminology, and an insufficiently charitable treatment of interested historical sources.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"suffering-divine-goodness","title":"Suffering and Divine Goodness","timebox":"40:16–48:58","score":{"pro":78,"con":74},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-evil-logical-compatibility","time":"46:57","role":"Supporting reply","words":"God and evil remain logically compatible if God could possibly possess morally sufficient reasons for permitting the world's suffering.","score":78,"critique":"Strongest feature: The response precisely states a modest modal defense: even possible morally sufficient reasons establish that God and evil are logically consistent, and the conclusion fits that limited premise. Principal limitation: Atkins's objection was evidential and comparative, not solely strict contradiction, so bare possibility neither explains the scale and distribution of innocent suffering nor neutralizes its probability-lowering force. Live burden: The affirmative must offer independently plausible categories of divine reasons and explain why apparently gratuitous cases remain reasonably expected under an all-loving deity, without transferring the entire evidential burden. Locked score: The strong band reflects exceptional internal coherence and explicit qualification, tempered by partial responsiveness, limited evidential development, and an incomplete answer to the simpler-absence comparison.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-suffering-c009-evil-evidence-against-god","time":"40:16","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"Innocent torment, accident, and disease are more simply expected without an all-loving God and therefore constitute evidence against that specified deity.","score":74,"critique":"Strongest feature: The argument presents suffering as probabilistic evidence rather than a strict contradiction, focuses on innocent torment, accident, and disease, and directly supports the naturalist route's central challenge to an all-loving deity. Principal limitation: Calling divine absence extraordinarily simple compresses the needed comparison between rival expectations, and the move does not answer the separate moral-grounding argument that happened to be its legally available earlier target. Live burden: Atkins must show why the amount and distribution of apparently gratuitous suffering are appreciably less expected under the specified theism after morally sufficient reasons are considered, without claiming observational omniscience. Locked score: The mixed band preserves credit for clarity, coherence, empirical salience, and indispensable burden contact, while reflecting limited responsiveness, implicit qualification, incomplete comparative development, and somewhat overstated explanatory confidence.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"explanatory-method-naturalism","title":"Scientific Explanation, Philosophy, and Divine Necessity","timebox":"26:25–99:33","score":{"pro":88,"con":67},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-method-science-neutral-premise","time":"98:32","role":"Diagnostic challenge","words":"Scientific evidence supports the neutral premise that the universe began, while philosophy draws theological implications without using God to fill scientific gaps.","score":88,"critique":"Strongest feature: The response separates a neutral premise informed by scientific evidence from the philosophical inference assigned theological significance, correcting the claim that the argument begins with faith or plugs an explanatory gap. Principal limitation: Calling the beginning premise textbook-neutral does not settle whether the cosmos, a wider sequence, or physical reality absolutely began, and the reply does not answer prospective natural explanations. Live burden: The affirmative must specify the scope and revisability of the premise, defend the metaphysical causal bridge independently, and explain why methodological naturalism neither forecloses nor validates the conclusion. Locked score: The very-strong band reflects precision, calibration, responsiveness, and charity, with restraint because subsidiary methodological success cannot substitute for completing the central cosmological inference.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-method-c004-simpler-natural-explanations","time":"26:25","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"No observed feature requires God when simpler natural explanations, or credible prospects for such explanations, remain available without adding a complex divine hypothesis.","score":70,"critique":"Strongest feature: This governing thesis directly addresses the motion-level requirement of divine necessity, explicitly preserves prospective scientific explanation, and offers parsimony as a coherent organizing consideration across cosmology, morality, and historical testimony. Principal limitation: Its undefined standard of simplicity and universal language about absolutely everything substantially exceed the displayed evidential support, making an acknowledged possibility of future explanation perform argumentative work that requires concrete comparative demonstration. Live burden: The naturalist must identify sufficiently developed alternatives for the central phenomena and explain why their evidential commitments are genuinely lighter, rather than treating scientific openness itself as affirmative evidence against theism. Locked score: The mixed band reflects exceptional debate-wide relevance and a recoverable qualified thesis, counterbalanced by materially unstable scope, limited warrant, and significant overstatement.","tags":[]}},{"pro":null,"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-method-c10-evidence-over-faith","time":"77:15","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Scientific inquiry advances cautiously through collected evidence, whereas untestable claims beginning from prior faith can explain everything verbally without producing genuine understanding.","score":63,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move articulates a methodological preference for cautious, evidence-guided inquiry and warns that an unrestricted explanatory hypothesis can accommodate outcomes without producing discriminating understanding or empirical accountability. Principal limitation: It depicts Craig's arguments as originating in faith despite assessable premises and probability claims, relying on contrasts among faith, demonstration, and science instead of examining the stated warrants individually. Live burden: Atkins must identify where each inference becomes insulated from evidence, distinguish prior commitment from invalidity, and address the cosmological and moral arguments rather than generalizing from the resurrection exchange. Locked score: The weak band recognizes methodological relevance and an evidential ideal, but deductions reflect low charity, overstated categorization, unstable scope, incomplete target contact, and supporting analysis.","tags":[]}}]}],"overall":{"pro":{"score":83,"strengths":["The cumulative case consistently connects cosmological, moral, and historical claims to distinct parts of the affirmative route instead of relying on a single inference.","The genealogy-validity reply identifies the decisive weakness in the evolutionary account by separating the origin of moral attitudes from their objective authority.","The methodological reply carefully distinguishes scientific support for a neutral premise from philosophical reasoning toward a theological conclusion.","The response to zero-energy language preserves the important conceptual distinction between a balanced physical state and literal nonbeing.","The resurrection reply appropriately treats miracle assessment as comparative explanation involving background evidence rather than automatic exclusion by prior improbability."],"blunders":[{"text":"The cosmological case compresses the crucial transition from a transcendent, immaterial cause to a personal agent without supplying a separate warrant for agency.","links":[]},{"text":"The moral argument effectively challenges evolutionary genealogy but does not fully compare divine grounding with the strongest available form of nontheistic moral realism.","links":[]},{"text":"The logical-compatibility defense answers strict inconsistency more successfully than it answers the evidential weight, scale, and distribution of innocent suffering.","links":[]},{"text":"The resurrection case does not sufficiently develop source reliability or compare concrete natural alternatives before claiming superior explanatory power.","links":[]}]},"con":{"score":73,"strengths":["Directly challenged the indispensable causal bridge by questioning whether intra-universe causal relations can govern an alleged beginning of physical reality.","Preserved an evidentially open distinction between finite individual universes and a potentially beginningless sequence without claiming that infinitude was established.","Presented innocent suffering as probabilistic evidence against an all-loving deity and identified concrete categories of apparently gratuitous harm.","Raised relevant reliability and comparative-evidence burdens against treating reported historical events as sufficient warrant for divine resurrection.","Offered evolutionary history and consequence-sensitive cognition as a substantive natural account of moral capacities and social distinctions."],"blunders":[{"text":"The debate-wide claim that simpler explanations exist or remain prospectively available for absolutely everything outran the supplied evidence and left simplicity undefined.","links":[]},{"text":"The moral genealogy explained the emergence of moral beliefs and behavior but did not answer the distinct question of objectively binding validity.","links":[]},{"text":"The resurrection critique characterized interested sources as political invention without detailed source analysis sufficient to establish fabrication or unreliability.","links":[]},{"text":"The faith-versus-evidence contrast underrepresented publicly assessable premises and arguments offered by the affirmative, weakening methodological charity.","links":[]}]}},"logicalExtension":{"pro":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that classical theism becomes cumulatively credible when a contingent finite cosmos, irreducible objective normativity, and historically situated resurrection evidence receive one coherent personal explanation that survives the strongest naturalistic objections without treating any single consideration as conclusive.","premises":["If physical reality has a finite and contingent beginning, its adequate explanation cannot simply be another temporally prior physical event; the explanatory candidate must be capable of grounding physical existence without depending upon the spacetime order being explained.","A personal explanation of cosmic origination gains comparative support only if agency intelligibly accounts for a timeless condition producing a finite effect, while an impersonal sufficient condition would otherwise require an account of why its effect was not equally timeless.","Evolutionary genealogy can explain why humans possess particular moral judgments without determining whether those judgments are true; if categorical reasons remain valid independently of preference, a satisfactory worldview must explain their authority as well as our capacity to recognize them.","A resurrection inference should compare the expected evidence under divine action with expectations under specified natural alternatives, after independently evaluating source dependence, partisan interest, the historical facts, and the background case for theism.","Apparently gratuitous suffering retains evidential force even when logical compatibility is established, so the cumulative theistic case must include independently plausible reasons for divine permission and accept that unresolved suffering reduces its overall explanatory advantage."],"conclusion":"Therefore, if these repaired cosmological, moral, historical, and evidential premises are jointly better supported than their principal naturalistic alternatives, classical theism is a reasonable cumulative explanation of reality, though each premise remains open to independent challenge and revision."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Explanatory unification without gap reasoning","text":"Theism can be framed as an inference to unification rather than a placeholder for missing science. Scientific accounts may describe cosmic development, biological moral cognition, and the transmission of historical reports, while a metaphysical proposal asks whether their deepest conditions share an explanation. The relevant test is not whether science has an unanswered question, but whether one personal foundation explains contingency, normativity, and purposive agency with fewer independent ultimate commitments than treating each domain as brute. This argument succeeds only comparatively and must include the theoretical cost of positing a divine mind."},{"title":"Agency as a contrastive origin explanation","text":"A timeless personal cause should not be inferred merely because impersonal causation feels inadequate. The stronger proposal is contrastive: an agent can possess a standing capacity while freely determining that a finite reality exists, whereas an impersonal set of eternally sufficient conditions appears to leave no internal contrast explaining a finite effect rather than an eternal one. This does not prove divine freedom, but it supplies a specific reason to prefer agency if a timeless transcendent cause is independently required. Naturalistic models can answer by denying timeless sufficiency or by supplying their own contrast-making structure."},{"title":"Normative authority and perfect goodness","text":"If objective duties are genuine categorical reasons, identifying God merely as a commander risks making morality arbitrary, while identifying goodness independently of God risks leaving divine grounding unnecessary. A stronger theistic account treats perfect goodness as essential to the divine nature and duties as reasons arising from the relation between finite persons and that necessarily good reality. This proposal must still explain how facts about divine nature generate reasons for action and why nontheistic normative realism is less satisfactory. Its value is to clarify the grounding claim rather than assume that divine commands automatically establish moral authority."}]},"con":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that the motion remains unestablished unless the affirmative demonstrates that cosmic existence, objective normativity, and the resurrection reports each require the specified personal God more strongly than disciplined naturalistic or agnostic alternatives.","premises":["A beginning of our observable cosmic history does not by itself establish that every explanatory principle governing that boundary is temporal causation, much less that its explanation is personal, immaterial, morally perfect, or unique.","An evolutionary and social genealogy can explain moral capacities, but the con case should remain neutral among several accounts of normative truth and argue only that the affirmative has not established exclusive divine grounding.","A singular miracle claim should be assessed through source independence, transmission, alternative explanations, background expectations, and comparative likelihoods rather than rejected merely because it is nonrepeatable or reported by interested witnesses.","Apparently gratuitous innocent suffering has evidential force when its quantity, distribution, and apparent independence from character-building purposes are less expected under an all-loving providence than under indifferent natural processes.","Methodological restraint requires comparing the explanatory reach, independent support, auxiliary assumptions, and predictive discrimination of rival hypotheses, not assuming either that future science will explain everything or that unexplained facts indicate God."],"conclusion":"Accordingly, the proportionate con conclusion is not that naturalism has explained everything, but that the presented cumulative case does not establish the specified God while significant causal, normative, historical, and evidential alternatives remain unresolved."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Explanatory-Specificity Test","text":"A hypothesis gains comparative support when it makes the observed evidence more expected than relevant alternatives do. The generic claim that a personal God created reality can accommodate many cosmic histories, moral psychologies, and distributions of suffering unless additional divine intentions are independently specified. Those flexible intentions risk reducing predictive discrimination. The con case can therefore ask, phenomenon by phenomenon, what distinctive observation the specified theism anticipated and why that observation was substantially less expected under nonpersonal alternatives."},{"title":"Cumulative Dependence Audit","text":"A cumulative case is strengthened only when its components provide at least partly independent support. Here the personal character inferred from cosmic origin, the moral character inferred from objective value, and the agency invoked for resurrection must be connected without quietly importing conclusions from one argument into another. If each bridge remains contested and the resurrection prior depends on already accepting the cosmic and moral conclusions, the apparent accumulation may be substantially dependent. The con side should demand an explicit map of those dependencies before treating several incomplete inferences as mutually reinforcing."}]}},"year":2011,"topicCategory":"science-design"};
